"(PDF) Structure-based identification of a novel NTPase from (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12895555_Structure-based_identification_of_a_novel_NTPase_from)\nciteturn529view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 1.8 years ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/12895555_Structure-based_identification_of_a_novel_NTPase_from\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1881\nL0: Article PDF Available\nL1: # Structure-based identification of a novel NTPase from\nL2: \nL3:   * August 1999\nL4:   * 【0†Nature Structural Biology】 6(7):691-6\nL5: \nL6: DOI:【1†10.1038/10745†dx.doi.org】\nL7: \nL8:   * Source\nL9:   * 【2†PubMed】\nL10: \nL11: Authors:\nL12: \nL13: 【3† 】\nL14: \nL15: 【3†Kwang Yeon Hwang】\nL16: \nL17:   * 【4†Korea University】\nL18: \nL19: 【5† 】\nL20: \nL21: 【5†Ji Hyung Chung】\nL22: \nL23:   * 【6†CHA University】\nL24: \nL25: 【7† 】\nL26: \nL27: 【7†Sung-Hou Kim】\nL28: \nL29: 【7†Sung-Hou Kim】\nL30: \nL31:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL32: \nL33: 【8† 】\nL34: \nL35: 【8†Ye Sun Han】\nL36: \nL37: 【8†Ye Sun Han】\nL38:   * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\nL39: \nL40: Show all 5 authors Hide\nL41: \nL42: [Image 0]\nL43: \nL44: 【9†Download full-text PDF】【10†Read full-text】\nL45: \nL46: 【11†Download full-text PDF】\nL47: \nL48: 【10†Read full-text】\nL49: \nL50: 【12†Download citation】\nL51: \nL52: Copy link Link copied\nL53: \nL54: * * *\nL55: \nL56: 【10† Read full-text】【12† Download citation】\nL57: \nL58: Copy link Link copied\nL59: \nL60: 【13†Citations (126)】【14†References (49)】【15†Figures (5)】\nL61: ## Abstract and Figures\nL62: Almost half of the entire set of predicted genomic products from Methanococcus jannaschii are classified as functionally unknown hypothetical proteins. We present a structure-based identification of the biochemical function of a protein with an as yet unknown function from a M. jannaschii gene, Mj0226. The crystal structure of Mj0226 protein determined at 2.2 A resolution reveals that the protein is a homodimer and each monomer folds into an elongated alpha/beta structure of a new fold family.\nL63: Comparisons of Mj0226 protein with protein structures in the database, however, indicate that one part of the protein is homologous to some of the nucleotide-binding proteins. Biochemical analysis shows that Mj0226 protein is a novel nucleotide triphosphatase that can efficiently hydrolyze nonstandard nucleotides such as XTP to XMP or ITP to IMP, but not the standard nucleotides, in the presence of Mg2+ or Mn2+ ions.\nL64: 【16† 】\nL65: \nL66: 【16†Statistics of crystallographic data for Mj0226 … 】\nL67: \nL68: 【17† 】\nL69: \nL70: 【17†Kinetic parameters of Mj0226 with various nucleotides … 】\nL71: \nL72: 【18† 】\nL73: 【18†Structure comparison of Mj0226 protein (red) with the C-terminaldomains of histidyl- (blue) and glycyl- (cyan) tRNA synthetases.b, Structure comparison of Mj0226 protein (red) with flavodoxin(blue). The ligand (AMPPNP or FMN) for each protein is shown in thick red. c, Comparison of topologies in four different proteins, Mj0226, histidyl-tRNAsynthetase domain, flavodoxin and GAR transformylase. … 】\nL74: \nL75: 【19† 】\nL76: 【19†Sequence alignment of Mj0226 with other homologous proteins. Secondary structures of Mj0226 are shown. The conserved residues from17 different proteins are boxed (yellow) and only 7 proteins are shown forclarity. … 】\nL77: \nL78: 【20† 】\nL79: \nL80: 【20†A |Fo| - |Fc| map showing theAMPPNP.The map was contoured at 3. b, A stereodiagram showingthe nucleotide binding region within Mj0226 protein. Each hydrogen bond betweenthe ligand (yellow) and residues (blue) are shown. … 】\nL81: \nL82: Figures - uploaded by 【3†Kwang Yeon Hwang】\nL83: Author content\nL84: \nL85: All figure content in this area was uploaded by Kwang Yeon Hwang\nL86: \nL87: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL88: \nL89: [Image 1: ResearchGate Logo]\nL90: \nL91: Discover the world's research\nL92: \nL93:   * 25+ million members\nL94:   * 160+ million publication pages\nL95:   * 2.3+ billion citations\nL96: \nL97: 【21†Join for free】\nL98: \nL99: Public Full-text 1\nL100: \nL101: Content uploaded by 【3†Kwang Yeon Hwang】\nL102: \nL103: Author content\nL104: \nL105: All content in this area was uploaded by Kwang Yeon Hwang\nL106: \nL107: Content may be subject to copyright.\nL108: \nL109: articles\nL110: Over the past few years, significant progress has been made in\nL111: \nL112: determining the entire genome sequence from several organisms.\nL113: \nL114: Currently, 15 microbial genomes have been fully sequenced, and\nL115: \nL116: 60 other genomes are being sequenced, including the human\nL117: \nL118: genome\nL119: \nL120: 1–3\nL121: \nL122: . One of the primary goals of such extensive genomic\nL123: \nL124: sequencing is to discover the molecular (biochemical and bio-\nL125: \nL126: physical) functions and cellular functions (resulting from the net-\nL127: working of molecular functions) of all the gene products in the\nL128: \nL129: nature structural biology • volume 6 number 7 • july 1999  691\nL130: \nL131: genomes and to understand their roles in cellular processes. A t\nL132: \nL133: the present time, the primary method of predicting the molecular\nL134: \nL135: and/or cellular functions of these gene products is to search for\nL136: \nL137: homologous protein or RNA sequences for which the functions\nL138: \nL139: are already known\nL140: \nL141: 4–5\nL142: \nL143: . However, analyses of complete genome\nL144: sequences from a number of organisms have rev ealed that, on\nL145: \nL146: average, about one-half of all gene products can be assigned a\nL147: \nL148: function based on sequence homologies, leaving a significant\nL149: \nL150: Structure-based identification of a novel\nL151: \nL152: NTPase from Methanococcus jannaschii\nL153: \nL154: Kwang Y eon Hwang\nL155: \nL156: 1,2\nL157: \nL158: , Ji Hyung Chung\nL159: \nL160: 1–3\nL161: \nL162: , Sung-Hou Kim\nL163: \nL164: 4\nL165: \nL166: , Y e Sun Han\nL167: \nL168: 2\nL169: \nL170: and Y unje Cho\nL171: \nL172: 2\nL173: \nL174: Almost half of the entire set of predicted genomic products from Methanococcus jannaschii are classified as\nL175: functionally unknown hypothetical proteins. We present a structure-based identification of the biochemical function\nL176: \nL177: of a protein with an as yet unknown function from a M. jannaschii gene, Mj0226. The crystal structure of Mj0226\nL178: \nL179: protein determined at 2.2 Å resolution reveals that the protein is a homodimer and each monomer folds into an\nL180: \nL181: elongated a/b structure of a new fold family. Comparisons of Mj0226 protein with protein structur es in the database,\nL182: however, indicate that one part of the protein is homologous to some of the nucleotide-binding proteins. Biochemical\nL183: \nL184: analysis shows that Mj0226 protein is a novel nucleotide triphosphatase that can efficiently hydrolyze nonstandard\nL185: \nL186: nucleotides such as XTP to XMP or ITP to IMP, but not the standar d nucleotides, in the presence of Mg 2+ or Mn 2+ ions.\nL187: \nL188: 1\nL189: \nL190: These authors contributed equally to this work.\nL191: \nL192: 2\nL193: Structural Biology Center, Kor ea Institute of Science and T echnology, P.O. Box 131, Cheongryang, Seoul 130-650,\nL194: \nL195: South Korea.\nL196: \nL197: 3\nL198: \nL199: Graduate School of Biotechnology, Kor ea University, Seoul, South Korea.\nL200: \nL201: 4\nL202: \nL203: Department of Chemistry and E.O. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory,\nL204: \nL205: University of California, Berkeley, California 94720, USA.\nL206: \nL207: Correspondence should be addressed to Y.C. email: yunje@sbc4.kist.re.kr\nL208: Fig. 1 a,A stereodiagram of the experimental (MIR) electron density map at 3.0 Å resolution. The map is contoured at 1 s, with the final model dis-\nL209: \nL210: played for comparison. b, Overall structure of Mj0226 protein dimer. The helices are colored blue, b-strands are violet and loops are green. The N-\nL211: \nL212: and C-terminus of the protein are labeled, and the helices and strands in one monomer are numbered. The secondary structure assignment was\nL213: based on the program Procheck 42. c, Overall structure of Mj0226 protein dimer rotated by 90° from the view in (b). d, A diagram showing the sol-\nL214: \nL215: vent-accessible surface (GRASP 43) of Mj0226 protein dimer. One monomer is colored blue and the other violet, and the AMPPNP is shown in yellow.\nL216: \nL217: Some of the conserved residues are labeled.\nL218: \nL219: a b\nL220: \nL221: c d\nL222: \nL223: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL224: \nL225: © 1999 Nature America Inc. • http://structbio.nature.com\nL226: \nL227: articles\nL228: fraction of genes coding for hypothetical proteins with no known\nL229: \nL230: functions\nL231: \nL232: 1,2,6–12\nL233: \nL234: . With recent advances in t echnology, software,\nL235: \nL236: and methods for three-dimensional structure determination, the\nL237: \nL238: structure-based prediction of molecular functions emerges as a\nL239: \nL240: viable method for ‘hypothetical proteins’\nL241: \nL242: 13–16\nL243: \nL244: , the proteins with\nL245: \nL246: no sequence homologies to those with known functions.\nL247: \nL248: Biochemical or biophysical (molecular) function of a protein is\nL249: tightly correlated to its three-dimensional structure, which is pri-\nL250: \nL251: marily governed by its amino acid sequence. Howev er, it is often\nL252: \nL253: observed that proteins with low sequence identities can fold into\nL254: \nL255: similar structures and have related functions\nL256: \nL257: 17–19\nL258: \nL259: . Furthermore, it\nL260: \nL261: has been shown recently that proteins with high sequence similar-\nL262: \nL263: ity (up to 50% sequence identity) could form completely different\nL264: \nL265: structures\nL266: \nL267: 20\nL268: \nL269: . Thus, the structural information of a hypothetical\nL270: protein may extend the limitations of sequence-based pr ediction\nL271: \nL272: of molecular function and play an impor-\nL273: \nL274: tant role in predicting its molecular func-\nL275: \nL276: tion. Here, we provide an example in\nL277: \nL278: which the structure determination fol-\nL279: \nL280: lowed by biochemical assays can elucidate\nL281: \nL282: a possible molecular function of a hypo-\nL283: \nL284: thetical protein.\nL285: \nL286: Overall structure\nL287: \nL288: The structure of a protein coded by a hypo-\nL289: \nL290: thetical gene of M. jannaschii, Mj0226, has\nL291: \nL292: been determined by the multiple isomor-\nL293: phous replacement (MIR) method and\nL294: \nL295: refined to a crystallographic R-factor of\nL296: \nL297: 18.9% at 2.2 Å resolution (Fig. 1 a; T able 1).\nL298: \nL299: Both gel filtration and dynamic light-scat-\nL300: \nL301: tering studies show that Mj0226 protein\nL302: \nL303: forms a dimer under physiological condi-\nL304: \nL305: tions (data not shown). Consistent with\nL306: \nL307: our biochemical analysis, the crystal struc-\nL308: \nL309: ture of Mj0226 protein reveals that the pro-\nL310: \nL311: tein is a homodimer. The monomer\nL312: \nL313: structure has an a/b fold that has not been\nL314: previously observed in other structures.\nL315: \nL316: The overall structure has an elongated\nL317: \nL318: shape consisting of two lobes connected by\nL319: \nL320: 692 nature structural biology • volume 6 number 6 • june 1999\nL321: \nL322: a b-sheet, and has dimensions of 27 Å ´34 Å ´50 Å (Fig. 1 b,c).\nL323: \nL324: Three long antiparallel b-strands (b3, b4 and b5) form a twisted\nL325: \nL326: b-sheet composed at the center of a monomer. In one lobe, one\nL327: \nL328: face of the central b-sheet is surrounded by several loops and\nL329: helices (a 3–a 7), whereas the other face is completely exposed t o\nL330: \nL331: solvent. The strand b 3 in this lobe joins a short strand b 4 in an\nL332: \nL333: antiparallel manner. In the other lobe, b 3 is joined by two short N-\nL334: \nL335: terminal  b-strands (b 1 and b 2) to form a fiv e-stranded b-sheet,\nL336: \nL337: and this sheet is surrounded by three a-helices (a 1, a 2 and a 8). A\nL338: \nL339: cleft 9 Å wide, 28 Å long and 11 Å deep is formed between the two\nL340: \nL341: lobes (Fig. 1 d). T wo monomers of Mj0226 are related by a non-\nL342: crystallographic two-fold axis that is parallel to the length of the\nL343: \nL344: central b-sheet. The dimeric interface is formed by the packing of\nL345: \nL346: portions of the helices a 2, a 3, a 4 and a loop between b 2 and a 2,\nL347: \nL348: and has a buried surface area of 1,887 Å\nL349: \nL350: 2\nL351: \nL352: . The interactions between\nL353: \nL354: the two monomers are primarily hydrophobic in nature (77% of\nL355: \nL356: the buried area is nonpolar). Several hydrophobic clusters are\nL357: \nL358: formed by the side chains of 10 nonpolar residues (Fig. 2). The\nL359: first hydrophobic cluster includes Leu 81 and Phe 84 from strand\nL360: \nL361: b 4, and Phe 91, V al 92 and T yr 88 from helix a 3. The second clus-\nL362: \nL363: ter consists of Pro 44 and Ile 46 from the loop between b 2 and a 2,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructure-based identification of a novel NTPase from Methanococcus jannaschii | Nature Structural & Molecular Biology (https://www.nature.com/articles/nsb0799_691.pdf)\nciteturn529view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/nsb0799_691.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.nature.com/articles/nsb0799_691?error=cookies_not_supported&code=f51ccb72-cd48-4823-a0fb-fe28dd5e01e7; Total lines: 363\nL0:   * 【0†View all journals】\nL1:   * 【1†Saved research】\nL2:   * 【2†Search】\nL3:   * 【3†Account†my-profile.springernature.com】 【4†Log in†idp.nature.com】\nL4: \nL5:   * 【5†Content Explore content】\nL6:   * 【6†About the journal】\nL7:   * 【7†Publish with us】\nL8:   * 【8†Subscribe】\nL9: \nL10:   * 【9†Sign up for alerts†journal-alerts.springernature.com】\nL11:   * 【10†RSS feed】\nL12: \nL13:   * Article\nL14:   * Published: July 1999\nL15: # Structure-based identification of a novel NTPase from Methanococcus jannaschii\nL16: \nL17:   * 【11†Kwang Yeon Hwang】^{【12†1】}^{ 【13†na1】},\nL18:   * 【14†Ji Hyung Chung】^{【12†1】,【15†2】}^{ 【13†na1】},\nL19:   * 【16†Sung-Hou Kim】^{【17†3】}^{ 【13†na1】},\nL20:   * 【18†Ye Sun Han】^{【12†1】} &\nL21:   * …\nL22:   * 【19†Yunje Cho】^{【12†1】}\nL23: Show authors\nL24: \nL25: 【20†Nature Structural & Molecular Biology】 volume 6, pages 691–696 (1999)\nL26: \nL27: 【21†Save article】\nL28: \nL29: 【1†View saved research】\nL30: \nL31:   * 498 Accesses\nL32: \nL33:   * 129 Citations\nL34: \nL35:   * 【22†Metrics details】\nL36: ## Abstract\nL37: Almost half of the entire set of predicted genomic products from Methanococcus jannaschii are classified as functionally unknown hypothetical proteins. We present a structure-based identification of the biochemical function of a protein with an as yet unknown function from a M. jannaschii gene, Mj0226. The crystal structure of Mj0226 protein determined at 2.2 Å resolution reveals that the protein is a homodimer and each monomer folds into an elongated α/β structure of a new fold family.\nL38: Comparisons of Mj0226 protein with protein structures in the database, however, indicate that one part of the protein is homologous to some of the nucleotide-binding proteins. Biochemical analysis shows that Mj0226 protein is a novel nucleotide triphosphatase that can efficiently hydrolyze nonstandard nucleotides such as XTP to XMP or ITP to IMP, but not the standard nucleotides, in the presence of Mg^{2+} or Mn^{2+} ions.\nL39: This is a preview of subscription content, 【23†access via your institution†wayf.springernature.com】\nL40: ## Access options\nL41: \nL42: 【23†Access th